  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Differentiating an exponential and natural logarithm functions.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Differentiate the following function.</p>
<p>\( \qquad \displaystyle R (w) = {@rw@}\)</p>
<p><b>Note:</b>
<ul>
  <li>\({@a@} {@log(b)@}\) can be typed as <code>a*log(b)</code></li>
  <li>Give your answer correctly to \(2\) decimal places where applicable</li>
</ul>
</p>
<p>\( \qquad \displaystyle R' (w) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>\( \qquad \displaystyle R'\left({@w_val@}\right) = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To differentiate the function \( \displaystyle R(w) = {@rw@}\), you'll need to use the rules of differentiation.</p>

<p><strong>1. Differentiate the first term \( \displaystyle {@rw1@}\):</strong></p>
<p>The derivative of \(  \displaystyle {@rw1@}\) with respect to \(w\) can be found using the chain rule. The chain rule states that if you have a function of the form \(a^u\), where \(a\) is a constant and \(u\) is a function of \(w\), then the derivative is \( \displaystyle \ln(a) \cdot a^u \cdot \frac{du}{dw}\). In this case, \(a = {@aa@}\) and \(u = w\).</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \frac{d}{dw}\left({@rw1@}\right) = {@diff_term1@} \cdot \frac{dw}{dw} = {@diff_term1@}\)</p>

<p><strong>2. Differentiate the second term \( \displaystyle {@rw2@}\):</strong></p>
<p>To differentiate \(  \displaystyle {@rw2@}\), we can use the chain rule and the fact that \( \displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left(\log_a(x)\right) = \frac{1}{x\ln(a)}\). So, we have:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{d}{dw}\left({@rw2@}\right) = {@diff_term2@} \cdot \frac{d}{dw}(w) =  {@diff_term2@} \)</p>

<p>Now, we can combine the derivatives of both terms to find the derivative of the entire function \(R(w)\):</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle R'(w) = \frac{d}{dw}\left({@rw1@}\right)+ \frac{d}{dw}\left({@rw2@}\right)\)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle R'(w) = {@ta1@} \)</p>

<p>So, the derivative of the function \(R(w) = {@rw@} \) is:</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle R'(w) = {@ta1@}  \)</p>

<p>To find \(\displaystyle R'\left({@w_val@}\right)\), plug in \(\displaystyle w = {@w_val@}\) into the derivative expression:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle R'\left({@w_val@}\right) = {@ta2@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
